| 1 | what-happened | On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who, like Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha, are in the United States and were not admitted. The October 1 order grants review and does not decide the case. The bond statute is the statute under which the government may release someone. | scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01: Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01.txt<br>uscode-2024-1226: may release the alien on; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1226.txt |
| 2 | what-happened | For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people present without admission could ask for bond. In July 2025 the government said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way in Barbosa da Cunha's case. On October 1 the Supreme Court agreed to decide the question. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Executive Branch practice over thirty years; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05: Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt |
| 3 | what-happened | The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. | uscode-2024-1225: in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1225.txt |
| 4 | what-happened | in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained | uscode-2024-1225: in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1225.txt |
| 5 | what-happened | The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization; he was already living here. So the court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. A bond hearing is a hearing on release. Release in his case followed a finding that he was not a danger and not a flight risk. The hearing did not itself require release. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. Under the Department of Homeland Security's July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. That memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: has never been arrested for or charged with a crime.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: The only aliens eligible for a custody determination and release on recognizance, bond, or conditional parole under INA § 236(a) are aliens admitted to the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt |
