| 7 | what-happened | Barbosa da Cunha entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested him while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied a bond hearing. A district court then ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk. If the government wins, people in his situation are held through the removal process: the Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he “must be detained during the removal process” with no opportunity for release on bond. Its memorandum says an applicant for admission may not be released except by parole. By the grant the dispute reached far beyond his case: on September 10 the Fourth Circuit wrote that “The debate has now reached every numbered United States court of appeals and has led to a circuit split.” | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: must be detained during the removal process; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The debate has now reached every numbered United States court of appeals and has led to a circuit split.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: divided panels in two other circuits have agreed with the government; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: they must be detained without bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt |
| 8 | what-happened | The government's petition asked the Court to wait on this case. It said the Court should hold this petition pending another petition, Lopez-Campos, which it called a "better vehicle" for resolving whether holding those people is lawful, and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. The October 1 order granted this petition. | doj-petition-26-104: The Court should hold the petition in this case pending the resolution of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Lopez-Campos is a better vehicle than this case for resolving the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens who are present in the country without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt |
