| 17 | where-this-sits | On March 6, 1997, a Federal Register rule said that people present without having been admitted or paroled would be eligible for bond and bond redetermination. The Second Circuit describes the executive practice that followed as thirty years old. In July 2025 the government changed that practice and said the detention statute, rather than the bond statute, is the detention authority for every applicant for admission. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant bond to people present in the United States without admission. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit held the opposite for someone already present who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, and it affirmed. On September 25 the full court refused rehearing. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented and argued that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted to deny rehearing and wrote that he agreed with that dissent. On October 1 the Supreme Court granted review. | fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06: Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.; data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Executive Branch practice over thirty years; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05: Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25: Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25: The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt |
