| 20 | where-this-sits | On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit affirmed the district court. It held that detention of a person in the United States after entering without inspection and admission, who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter, follows the bond statute, not mandatory detention. Judge Cabranes agreed and filed a separate opinion. The appeal was argued April 6. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Decided: April 28, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Argued: April 6, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Judge Cabranes concurs in the judgment and opinion of the Court and files a separate opinion.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
