checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-2793-The IIRIRA extended the mandatory detention provisions to additional classes of inadmissible and deportable aliens but provided an exception for certain witnesses. It also allowed the Attorney General the option of a transition period for implementation of mandatory detention. The Service exercised this discretion and implemented the transition period custody rules on October 9, 1996, effective for 1 year. This interim rule amends the regulations to comply with the amended Act by removing the release from custody provisions for aliens who may no longer be released. These amendments to the regulations will take effect upon the termination of the transition period. As for non- criminal aliens, the rule reflects the new $1,500 minimum bond amount specified by IIRIRA. Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-2794-Several commenters complained that the Service has no national standards of detention. They stated that policies, practices, and decisions regarding outside communication are bewildering, arbitrary, and inconsistent. Consistent with its focus on providing safe, secure, and humane detention environments, the Service has implemented detention facility improvements and has set as a
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-2795-goal the accreditation of each of its facilities. The Krome Service Processing Center (SPC) has received accreditation with commendation from the Joint Commission of Healthcare Organizations (JCHO), the most prestigious medical accreditation that can be awarded. Currently, six SPCs are accredited by the National Commission on Correctional Health Care (NCCHC), and accreditation is pending at the remaining three SPCs. The Denver contract facility is also NCCHC accredited. Six contract facilities have American Correctional Association (ACA) accreditation and two others have begun the accreditation process.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log:2796:Several commenters stated that the Service should require ACA standards in local detention facilities used. Approximately 46 percent of the detention space used by the Service is with state and local facilities. Formal ACA accreditation of a state or local facility is a matter for the state or local government. The Service could not meet its detention requirements by using only facilities that have been formally accredited. The Service has established its own rigorous inspection program that uses ACA standards for evaluation of a facility. The Service will not use a facility that fails to pass our inspection.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-2797-Several commenters stated that § 236 of the proposed rule as written is a reversal of long established procedure that provides that a noncriminal alien is presumptively eligible for release. The Service has been strongly criticized for its failure to remove aliens who are not detained. A recent report by the Department of Justice Inspector General shows that when aliens are released from custody, nearly 90 percent abscond and are not removed from the United States. The mandate of Congress, as evidenced by budget enhancements and other legislation, is increased detention to ensure removal. Accordingly, because the Service believes that the regulation as written is consistent with the intent of Congress, the interim rule has not modified the proposed rule in this regard.
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-2798-Several commenters noticed a discrepancy between the discussion in the supplementary information and the substance of § 236.1(c)(5) of the proposed regulation. The supplementary information stated the Department’s intended approach, and clause (i) of the proposed regulation was in error. Accordingly, the interim rule removes paragraph (c)(5)(i) of § 236.1 and renumbers the remaining paragraphs (c)(5)(ii), (iii), and (iv). The effect of this change is that inadmissible aliens, except for arriving aliens, have available to them bond redetermination
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-2799-hearings before an immigration judge, while arriving aliens do not. This procedure maintains the status quo regarding release decisions for aliens in proceedings, as discussed in the supplementary information of the proposed regulation.
--
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-3015-68:          <p>The Court's list of cases set for argument is dated October 1, 2026. This case is on it with a grant date of October 1. That line does not set a day for argument.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="As of October 1, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /></p>
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-3016-101:        <p data-layer="narrative">Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted to deny rehearing and wrote that he agreed with Judge Menashi's dissent, the argument that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25" passage="The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing." /></p>
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log-3017-109:          <p>In July 2025 the government changed its practice. The opinion cites immigration enforcement guidance dated July 8, 2025. The petition names that guidance as Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and says applicants for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. Divided panels in the Fifth and Eighth Circuits agreed with the government.<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)" /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="Todd M. Lyons, Acting Director of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, Interim Guidance Regarding Detention Authority for Applicants for Admission (July 8, 2025)." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="may not be released from ICE custody” except by “parole”" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494, 508 (5th Cir. 2026)" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128, 1138 (8th Cir. 2026)" /></p>
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log:3018:118:          <p>The signed memorandum, a one-page scan, is addressed from Commissioner Rodney S. Scott. It says the department's position is that applicants for admission are subject to mandatory detention and may not be released except by parole.<Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="FROM: Rodney S. Scott" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="SUBJECT: Detention of Applicants for Admission" /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="subject to mandatory detention under INA" /></p>
checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r0-entailment.log:3019:125:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">This case has a grant date of October 1 and no argument day on the list of cases set for argument. The October 2 docket's last disposition is the grant, and later filings go through the electronic system. The question the Court accepted is the statute. The government's petition says a petition from the Sixth Circuit in Lopez-Campos also presents whether applying the detention statute comports with due process. The Second Circuit said it need not rely on constitutional avoidance, the practice of reading a statute so as not to decide a constitutional problem. The petition says the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining him without a bond hearing. A petition from the Fifth Circuit in Buenrostro-Mendez was distributed for the September 28 conference, then rescheduled, and those proceedings list no grant. The Sixth Circuit petition was distributed for that same conference, and its proceedings list no grant.<Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA" /><Cite s="scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01" passage="Granted: 10/1/26" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Petition GRANTED." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-02" passage="Subsequent party and amicus filings in the case should now be submitted through" /><Cite s="scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01" passage="Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we need not rely here upon the doctrine of constitutional avoidance." /><Cite s="doj-petition-26-104" passage="the Second Circuit did not reach a standalone holding on the constitutionality of detaining respondent without a bond hearing" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-03" passage="DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit" /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="DISTRIBUTED for Conference of 9/28/2026." /><Cite s="scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03" passage="Rescheduled." /></p>

