300:Had Congress intended only to eliminate the availability of parole for those already mandatorily detained under Section 1225, it would have either done so through an amendment to that section of the statute, or by adding an additional exception to those already enumerated in 8 U.S.C. § 1182(d)(5), which governs parole. Further undermining the Government’s argument, Congress did in fact amend Section 1225(b) when it passed the Laken Riley Act to permit state attorneys general to sue the Secretary of Homeland Security to enforce the act. Id. § 1225(b)(3). Yet, Congress did not address mandatory detention for noncitizens who are present in the country unlawfully in that subsection. Second, the Government asserts that Congress included the Laken Riley Act’s Section 1226 amendment because, at the time, the Executive Branch was improperly providing noncitizens like the Petitioners with bond hearings rather than mandatorily detaining them. This argument exposes the flaws in the Government’s position, however. If Congress sought to correct the Executive Branch’s misunderstanding, it surely would have done so in a more explicit manner. In interpreting statutes, “Congress’[s] failure to repeal or revise [the statute] in the face of such administrative interpretation [is] persuasive evidence that that interpretation is the one intended by Congress.” CBS, Inc. v. FCC, 453 U.S. 367, 385 (1981) (quoting Zemel v. Rusk, 381 U.S. 1, 11 (1965)). Far from informing the Executive Branch that it was acting in defiance of legislative command, the passage of the Laken Riley Act affirms the longstanding practice of detaining noncitizens like the Petitioners under Section 1226. The canons of surplusage and meaningful variation, therefore, counsel that Section 1226 is the best reading of the statute.
