464-5. Petitioners’ surplusage objection Petitioners object that this reading is fatally flawed because it renders “seeking admission” and “applicant for admission” duplicative in Section 1225(b)(2)(A). That argument lacks force. As the Supreme Court has acknowledged, “[t]he anti-surplusage canon is useful, but it is ‘subordinate to the cardinal canon that a legislature says in a statute what it means and means in a statute what it says there.’” Mullin v. Al Otro Lado, 146 S. Ct. 2079, 2093 (2026) (quoting U.S. Postal Serv. v. Konan, 146 S. Ct. 736, 746 (2026)); see id. at 2092 (“The anti-surplusage canon is not an iron rule.”). “[E]ven excellent writers do not always trim every unnecessary word, and the same is true of Congress.” Id. at 2092. “Sometimes the better overall reading of the statute contains some redundancy.” Rimini Street, Inc. v. Oracle USA, Inc., 139 S. Ct. 873, 881 (2019). So it is here. Section 1225(b)(2)(A) uses different words to refer to synonymous concepts. Cf. Tyler v. Cain, 533 U.S. 656, 664 (2001) (“Congress, needless to say, is permitted to use synonyms in a statute.”). The statute mentions the same alien twice: once in the prefatory clause as “an alien who is an applicant for admission” and once in the operative clause as “an alien seeking admission.”4 As previously discussed, an applicant is one who seeks. Because the terms’ ordinary meanings overlap, there is some redundancy between them. But as the Supreme Court has repeatedly observed when interpreting the
