it sounds constitutional alarms that would warrant its rejection. See
Biden v. Nebraska, 600 U.S. 477, 509 (2023) (Barrett, J., concurring) (“[I]f
the better reading leads to a disfavored result (like provoking a
serious constitutional question), the court will adopt an inferior-but-
tenable reading to avoid it.”). Petitioner is protected by the Fifth
Amendment’s Due Process Clause, requiring any civil detention to be
“nonpunitive in purpose and effect.” Zadvydas, 533 U.S. at 690; see
also Velasco Lopez v. Decker, 978 F.3d 842, 850 (2d Cir. 2020) (“[T]he Due
Process Clause covers noncitizens, whether their presence here is
lawful, unlawful, temporary, or permanent.”). While noncitizens can
be detained temporarily to “give[] immigration officials time to
